  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">SUSTAINED PROTECTION SUPPORTING POPULATION GROWTH AND RANGE EXPANSION OF ELEPHANTS IN THE EASTERN NSUMBU-MWERU ECOSYSTEM, ZAMBIA</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">THE PURPOSE OF THIS PROJECT IS TO SUPPORT AN ELEPHANT CONSERVATION EFFORT IN ZAMBIAS NSUMBU NATIONAL PARK AND SURROUNDING GAME MANAGEMENT AREAS BY STRENGTHENING ELEPHANT PROTECTION AND BIOLOGICAL MONITORING IN SUPPORT OF ELEPHANT POPULATION GROWTH AND RANGE EXPANSION. NSUMBU NATIONAL PARKS ELEPHANTS ARE GENETICALLY DISTINCT, AND THE LAST ELEPHANTS FOUND ANYWHERE ALONG THE SHORES OF LAKE TANGANYIKA. THIS POPULATION OF APPROXIMATELY 200 INDIVIDUALS CONSTITUTES THE SURVIVING REMNANTS OF A MUCH WIDER HISTORIC ELEPHANT POPULATION THAT ONCE ROAMED THROUGHOUT THE REGION. THIS PROJECT IS INTENDED TO CONSERVE AFRICAN SAVANNA ELEPHANTS AND THEIR HABITAT BY ADDRESSING THE IMPACTS OF POACHING. ACTIVITIES INCLUDE: (1) PROVISIONING ADEQUATE SUPPLIES, INCLUDING, RATIONS, FUEL (AND ASSOCIATED VEHICLE MAINTENANCE), FIRST AID KITS, UNIFORMS, SLEEPING BAGS, AND COMMUNICATIONS EQUIPMENT FOR ANTI-POACHING PATROLS (2) CONDUCTING RANGER REFRESHER TRAININGS TWICE EACH YEAR (3) MAINTAINING DRY SEASON ROAD ACCESS IN THE EASTERN SECTOR OF THE LANDSCAPE AND (4) FACILITATING REGULAR INFORMATION SHARING BETWEEN ZAMBIA AND TANZANIA REGARDING CROSS-BORDER ELEPHANT MOVEMENTS AND ILLEGAL ACTIVITIES.</narrative>
  </description>
